(Reading elapsed while the timer is still running, before Stop. The
 still-running path samples the clock into a stack timespec; before the
 fix it read only the seconds half and threw the nanoseconds away, so a
 200 millisecond wait read 0 — or 1000 when it crossed a second boundary.
 It must now read roughly 200. The lower bound sits under the requested
 wait and the ceiling is loose. Stopping afterwards must give the same
 order of magnitude, proving the stored-end and running paths agree.)

a timer called clock.
Start the clock.
Wait 200 milliseconds.
a number called 'milliseconds while running' is the clock's elapsed in milliseconds.
If 'milliseconds while running' is greater than 100 and 'milliseconds while running' is less than 800 then,
    Print "running ok".
Otherwise,
    Print "running bad".
Stop the clock.
a number called 'milliseconds after stop' is the clock's elapsed in milliseconds.
If 'milliseconds after stop' is greater than 100 and 'milliseconds after stop' is less than 800 then,
    Print "stopped ok".
Otherwise,
    Print "stopped bad".